/-!
# Condition (2) implies condition (1) in Tag 063Y

For a morphism of first-order thickenings `(f, f') : (X ⊂ X') → (S ⊂ S')` with `f` flat,
[Stacks Project, Tag 063Y] asserts the equivalence of

* **(1)** `f'` is flat and `X = S ×_{S'} X'`, and
* **(2)** the canonical map `f^* 𝒞_{S/S'} ⟶ 𝒞_{X/X'}` is an isomorphism.

The implication (1) ⟹ (2) is
`Stacks.Thickenings.isIso_firstOrderThickeningConormalSheafMap` of
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.MoreMorphisms.FirstOrderThickeningConormalMap`.  This file proves
the converse and assembles the equivalence the tag asserts.

Both clauses of condition (1) are affine-local, and the file reduces them to one statement about a
pair of affine opens: for affine opens `U ⊆ X'` and `V ⊆ S'` with `f'(U) ⊆ V`, writing
`J ⊆ Γ(S', V)` and `I ⊆ Γ(X', U)` for the ideals cutting out `S` and `X`, the chart data is that
`Γ(S', V) ⟶ Γ(X', U)` is flat and that `I` is generated by the image of `J`.  That is the shape in
which the commutative algebra of the tag — `Ideal.bijective_fibreTensorSMulHomToKer_iff` of
`StacksFrontier.RingTheory.Flat.SquareZeroConormal` — delivers its conclusion, and condition (2),
read over such a pair of charts, is what feeds it.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Thickenings.flat_and_isPullback_iff_isIso_firstOrderThickeningConormalSheafMap`:
  **[Stacks Project, Tag 063Y] for schemes**, the equivalence of (1) and (2).
* `Stacks.Thickenings.flat_and_isPullback_of_isIso_firstOrderThickeningConormalSheafMap`:
  **(2) ⟹ (1) of [Stacks Project, Tag 063Y] for schemes**, the direction this file supplies.
* `Stacks.Thickenings.ideal_eq_and_flat_of_isIso_immersionConormalSheafMap`: the chart data read
  off condition (2) over a compatible pair of affine opens.
* `Stacks.Thickenings.flat_and_isPullback_of_affine`: **the glueing step**.  Given the chart data
  on every compatible pair of affine opens, `f'` is flat and the square is cartesian.
* `Stacks.Thickenings.flat_and_ker_eq_of_addEquiv_chart` and
  `Stacks.Thickenings.flat_and_ker_eq_of_extendScalars_chart`: the commutative algebra of the
  tag in the vocabulary a chart produces — an additive bijection out of a tensor product, and
  out of an extension of scalars.
* `Stacks.Conormal.exists_affineModel_ker`: the affine model of a closed immersion over an affine
  open, presented by the kernel of its map on sections rather than by an unnamed ideal.
* `Stacks.IdealSheaf.ideal_comap_of_isAffineOpen`: the ideal of the inverse image of an ideal
  sheaf on an affine open `U` of the source lying over an affine open `V` of the target is
  generated by the image of the ideal on `V` under `f.appLE V U`.  Unlike
  `Stacks.IdealSheaf.ideal_comap_of_isAffineHom` this needs no affineness of the morphism, only a
  pair of affine opens.
* `ModuleCat.ExtendScalars.tensorAddEquiv`, `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.app_pullbackSection`
  and `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Modules.bijective_app_of_isIso`: the three general facts the
  chart computation needs — that extension of scalars along a ring map is the tensor product over
  the algebra that map defines, that a morphism out of a pullback evaluated on a pulled-back
  section is the restriction of its transpose, and that an invertible morphism of modules is
  bijective on sections.

## Implementation notes

Neither of the two geometric statements uses the thickening hypotheses: the glueing works for an
arbitrary commutative square of closed immersions.  The square-zero conditions enter only in the
commutative algebra, where they are hypotheses of
`Ideal.bijective_fibreTensorSMulHomToKer_iff`.

Flatness of a morphism of schemes is by definition the flatness of `f.appLE V U` for every pair
of affine opens, so that half is the chart data verbatim.  Cartesianness is
`AlgebraicGeometry.isPullback_of_isClosedImmersion`, whose hypothesis is the equality of ideal
sheaves `(j.ker).comap f' = i.ker`; an equality of ideal sheaves may be tested on a family of
affine opens covering the source, and the affine opens lying over an affine open of the target
form such a family, where `Stacks.IdealSheaf.ideal_comap_of_isAffineOpen` turns the inverse image
into the image of the ideal downstairs.

The chart-level input to the commutative algebra is taken as an abstract *additive* equivalence
pinned down on the elementary tensors `b ⊗ x` with `b` in the image of `Γ(X', U)`, rather than as
bijectivity of the canonical map `Ideal.fibreTensorSMulHomToKer`.  That is the form a chart of the
conormal map produces: the identification of the sections of a pullback with an extension of
scalars is itself pinned down only on elementary tensors, and it is linear over `Γ(X, i⁻¹U)`, not
over `Γ(X', U)`, so no single scalar ring makes the whole chain linear.  The elementary tensors
span because `Γ(X', U) ⟶ Γ(X, i⁻¹U)` is surjective, so the abstract equivalence agrees with the
canonical map and bijectivity transfers.  The quotient `Γ(S', V) ⧸ J` that the commutative algebra
is stated over is identified with `Γ(S, j⁻¹V)` inside the proof, flatness over it coming from
flatness over `Γ(S, j⁻¹V)` through `Module.Flat.trans`.

`Stacks.Thickenings.flat_and_ker_eq_of_extendScalars_chart` is stated for bare ring maps and
builds every `Algebra` and `IsScalarTower` instance it needs from them.  That is what lets the
chart computation hand over `AlgebraicGeometry.affinePullbackSectionsBaseChangeIso` untouched: the
`Module Γ(S, j⁻¹V) Γ(X, i⁻¹U)` instance carried by `ModuleCat.extendScalars` is
`Module.compHom _ _`, which agrees with `Algebra.toModule` only for an `Algebra` structure built
by `RingHom.toAlgebra`, and the comparison is then definitional.  Stating the lemma with an
`Algebra` instance argument instead would make `ModuleCat.ExtendScalars.tensorAddEquiv` false by
`rfl` and force a transport.

